Streams and optional together.

The two types that close this module are not neighbours by accident. They're the same idea at two scales. A stream is a pipeline over many elements; an optional is a pipeline over zero or one. They share map, filter, and flat-map, they share the same laziness of intent, and they hand off to each other at the ends of a computation. This section is where the whole module clicks together.

First, notice that they speak the same language. Put their operations side by side, and the family resemblance is exact. Stream dot map dot filter transforms and keeps, over many elements. Optional dot map dot filter transforms and keeps, over zero or one. An optional is really just a stream that holds at most one value. That's why the reflexes you built for streams transfer wholesale: map transforms what's inside, filter can empty it, and flat-map avoids nesting. Learn the shape once, and it works at both scales.

Now, the natural handoff. The two meet most often at a terminal. A stream searches; the result might not exist; so the honest return type is optional. Find-first, min, max, and the one-argument reduce are exactly this junction — stream in, optional out — and you finish by leaving the box. So picture this: employees dot stream, filter to the engineering department, max by salary — that gives an optional employee, because the department might be empty. Then map to the name — an optional string, only if one was found. Then or-else, "no engineers". The whole computation — search, then extract, then default — reads as one sentence, and the empty case is handled by construction, never by a forgotten null check.

Now the other direction: flat-map turns optionals back into a stream. Going the other way, you often have a stream of things, each of which might yield a value — so you get an optional per element. Mapping gives you a clumsy stream of optionals. What you actually want is just the values that are present. And because an optional behaves like a zero-or-one stream, flat-map flattens it perfectly — the empties vanish, the values pass through. So users dot stream, map to each user's email, giving a stream of optional emails — awkward. Then flat-map with optional-dot-stream: that turns each empty into zero elements and each present value into one. Then to-list. You're left with a stream of just the present emails — no filter-is-present, then map-get dance. Optional dot stream is the bridge that makes this seamless.

Let me close on the module in one throughline. Step back, and the whole of module seven is a single story about describing a computation, instead of performing it. A lambda made behaviour a value — that was module six. A stream threads those values through a pipeline over many elements. Laziness lets the pipeline fuse its stages and short-circuit. Collectors assemble the result; reduce folds it; and parallel streams run the very same description across cores. And optional carries that discipline down to the zero-or-one case, so a result that might not exist becomes a type, not a null waiting to fail. Data in, a description of the transform in the middle, a value out — with the empty case honest at every step. That is functional Java. And it's the style the rest of your Java will increasingly be written in.
